  <h2>Presets as a Starting Point</h2>

  <p>
    If you do not want to start from scratch, pick a preset from the dropdown, such as Rock, and fine-tune individual bands from there. Your own settings can be saved and reset with one click. The compressor comes with ready-made templates in four groups:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Basic:</strong> Gentle, Medium, Heavy — from soft smoothing to strong densification.</li>
    <li><strong>Genre:</strong> Rock, Pop, Electro, Jazz, Hip-Hop, Classical.</li>
    <li><strong>Instrument:</strong> Vocal, Drums, Bass, Podcast.</li>
    <li><strong>Mastering:</strong> Master and Limiter for the final polish.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The Compressor: Even Loudness Without Pumping</h2>

  <p>
    A compressor lowers loud passages and leaves quiet parts alone. The result sounds denser and louder without clipping. The 19-Band EQ Pro offers the five classic parameters:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Threshold:</strong> the level at which the compressor starts working, e.g. -30 dB.</li>
    <li><strong>Ratio:</strong> how much the signal is reduced. 4:1 is a solid all-round value.</li>
    <li><strong>Knee:</strong> how soft the transition into compression is.</li>
    <li><strong>Attack:</strong> how fast the compressor reacts. Short values like 3 ms catch transients.</li>
    <li><strong>Release:</strong> how fast it lets go again, around 250 ms for music.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The level meter shows input and output side by side in dBFS. You can see immediately how much the compressor is actually catching, and whether the signal still has headroom.
  </p>

