  <div class="tip-box">
    <p><strong>Tip:</strong> less is more. Cutting usually sounds more natural than boosting. If a voice sounds muffled, first gently lower the range around 200 to 450 Hz instead of boosting the highs heavily.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Typical Use Cases</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Podcast post-production:</strong> Podcast preset, a slight lift at 2 to 3 kHz, compressor Medium — the voice sits up front and stays evenly loud.</li>
    <li><strong>Music for headphones:</strong> a bit more sub-bass at 45 to 63 Hz, highs at 9 to 13.5 kHz for brilliance.</li>
    <li><strong>Refreshing old recordings:</strong> reduce noise above 13.5 kHz, lift the mids, Limiter as the final step.</li>
    <li><strong>Matching loudness:</strong> compressor Master or Limiter to bring several tracks to a similar level.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Privacy: Everything Stays in Your Browser</h2>

  <p>
    The equalizer processes audio entirely locally using your browser's Web Audio API. No file is uploaded to a server, not even for export. That makes the tool suitable for unreleased recordings and confidential interviews.
  </p>

